{
  "$comment": "The open-licence serif candidates F-275 measured against the drawn wordmark, pinned so the run can be reproduced. THE FILES ARE NOT COMMITTED — this feature measures and F-226 ships, so a font binary here would be a licence obligation with no reader. What is committed is what a second person needs to fetch the same twenty files and get the same numbers: the family, the exact file taken, its size and sha256, its URL, and whether an OFL.txt sits beside it in the source tree. Scores are in docs/design/R9-MOCKUP-FIDELITY.md section 5.",
  "feature": "F-275",
  "fetchedAt": "2026-09-16",
  "source": "https://github.com/google/fonts, ofl/ tree",
  "sourceCommit": "1ac2012c34919f5fa2675aacf723fa98edb30b5f",
  "$sourceCommitComment": "The URLs below point at the main branch, which moves. This is the commit main pointed at when the files were fetched; a reproduction should read the files at this sha.",
  "$chosenComment": "Chosen by the implementing session for RESEMBLANCE to the drawn letterforms rather than by popularity — a transitional or old-style serif with a large x-height and moderate-to-high stroke contrast, which is what 01 draws. Gelasio is metric-compatible with Georgia and Tinos with Times New Roman, the two faces F-220 measured closest among installed ones. It is an agent's shortlist of twenty, not the open-licence field.",
  "$licenceComment": "Nineteen of the twenty carry an OFL.txt beside the font in the source tree. Tinos does not: its METADATA.pb declares license \"OFL\" but no licence text sits in ofl/tinos, so its terms are unconfirmed from this source and it is excluded from any claim about the set being OFL. It was scored (0.609 on 01, 26th of 109) and is not a candidate to ship until that is settled.",
  "$variableComment": "A variable file is scored at each named instance GDI+ exposes, so twenty families became 89 faces and, with the twenty installed ones, 109 candidates per run.",
